---
name: branch-merge
description: Rebase a branch onto its base in the branch's worktree, resolve the conflicts, then land it into the base checkout.
model: claude
effort: high
budget:
  maxTurns: 40
  timeout: 30m
setup:
  cwd: "{{worktree}}"
prompt:
  system: >-
    You are running inside the git worktree {{worktree}}, which has branch
    {{branch}} checked out. Rebase {{branch}} onto {{base}} and resolve every
    conflict the rebase stops on, keeping each commit's own intent on top of
    what {{base}} already contains. Do not push, do not abort the rebase, and
    never touch the main checkout at {{repo}}: once your work verifies, gavel
    lands the branch there for you.
permissions:
  # Edits stay in the worktree; git is limited to inspecting, staging and
  # driving the rebase forward. Patterned Bash rules only bind on Claude,
  # which is why the model is pinned above.
  mode: acceptEdits
  tools:
    Read: allow
    Glob: allow
    Grep: allow
    Edit: allow
    Write: allow
    Bash(git status:*): allow
    Bash(git diff:*): allow
    Bash(git log:*): allow
    Bash(git show:*): allow
    Bash(git add:*): allow
    Bash(git rm:*): allow
    Bash(git commit:*): allow
    Bash(git commit --amend:*): deny
    Bash(git rebase:*): allow
    Bash(git -c core.editor=true rebase:*): allow
    Bash(gavel test:*): allow
    Bash(gavel lint:*): allow
    Bash(make build:*): allow
    Bash(git rebase --abort:*): deny
    Bash(git rebase -i:*): deny
    Bash(git rebase --interactive:*): deny
    Bash(git push:*): deny
    Bash(git reset:*): deny
    Bash(git merge:*): deny
    Bash(git checkout:*): deny
    Bash(git switch:*): deny
    Bash(git branch:*): deny
    Bash(git worktree:*): deny
workflow:
  verify:
    maxIterations: 3
    # Run in the worktree, after each turn. A failing check is fed back to the
    # agent with its output tail.
    commands:
      # No rebase is still stopped on a commit.
      - test ! -d "$(git rev-parse --git-path rebase-merge)" && test ! -d "$(git rev-parse --git-path rebase-apply)"
      # The branch now sits on top of the base.
      - |-
        git merge-base --is-ancestor {{shq base}} HEAD
      # No conflict markers survived into the rebased commits.
      - |-
        git diff --check {{shq base}}...HEAD
      # Every fix is committed: landing only carries commits.
      - test -z "$(git status --porcelain)"
  after:
    # Runs once, in the main checkout, only after verify passed. Rebasing made
    # the history linear, so incremental landing is a plain fast-forward.
    # Commands carrying user values are block scalars (a quoted value holding
    # ": " or " #" would break the YAML), and branch with shell rather than
    # Handlebars blocks so the frontmatter stays valid YAML before rendering.
    - when: onVerify
      cwd: "{{repo}}"
      commands:
        - |-
          case {{shq mode}} in
            squash) git merge --squash {{shq branch}} ;;
            incremental) git merge --ff-only {{shq branch}} ;;
            *) echo "mode must be squash or incremental, got "{{shq mode}} >&2; exit 1 ;;
          esac
        - |-
          [ {{shq mode}} != squash ] || if [ -n {{shq message}} ]; then git commit -m {{shq message}}; else git commit --no-edit; fi
        - |-
          git worktree remove {{shq worktree}}
        - |-
          git branch -D {{shq branch}}
---
## Rebase `{{branch}}` onto `{{base}}`

{{#if conflicts}}
Landing `{{branch}}` into `{{base}}` failed on these files:

{{#each conflicts}}
- `{{this}}`
{{/each}}

Expect the rebase to stop on them; later commits may conflict on other files too.

{{/if}}
1. Run `git rebase {{base}}`.
2. Each time the rebase stops on a conflict:
   - Resolve every `<<<<<<<` / `=======` / `>>>>>>>` hunk so the commit keeps its own intent on top of `{{base}}`. Leave no markers behind.
   - `git add` (or `git rm`) each resolved file.
   - Continue with `git -c core.editor=true rebase --continue`.
   - Use `git rebase --skip` only when the commit became empty because `{{base}}` already contains its change.
3. When the rebase has finished, run the project's build and tests. Fix any breakage the rebase introduced and commit the fix as a new commit with `git commit` — no amending, no squashing.
4. Finish with a clean worktree. Do not push, merge, reset, or switch branches.
